Z||000000||88formula||05-19-2000||10:32 PM||||I have searched the archives all over and the same question comes up but never gets answered. What are the performance numbers for you fieros with the 3.2-stroker engines in 1/4-mile times? There are a lot of you guy’s out there with the 3.2 so you must have brought it to the track by now?<P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 05-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||05-19-2000||10:34 PM||||I am still collecting the pieces for my 3.4 conversion. A special thanks to Spyder for the 4.10 geared 4 speed. When I get all the bugs worked out, I will post some times.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||05-20-2000||11:07 AM||||The 3.4-liter engines are not the same as the 3.1/3.2 engines. The 3.4 engines have more torque and can only rev to 5500 RPM, which stinks. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Eric||05-20-2000||01:59 PM||||My best quarter mile is 16.4 at 80mph.  The car is an 88 GT with a five speed.  I may have been easier than I could have on the car, but I don't think it could do much better than high 15s with any driver.  Right now the car has no top end horsepower probably because the exhaust is messed up really bad.  I won't expect more than mid 15s and 7.0 0-60 (8.3 right now).  Torque is noticably better.  Pulls hard from any rpm, but not that hard.  You already know what kind of goodies I have on this engine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GT Bastard||05-20-2000||07:06 PM||||geez man... that's worse than stock. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||05-20-2000||07:52 PM||||Eric, I sent you e-mail on how to check for a clogged exhaust. What did you find?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||05-20-2000||07:59 PM||||I haven't tried it.  Once I get my new y-pipe, I'll just replace everything down the line.  I can visually see the problem/problems.  The cat could be clogged too.  Everything must go!<P>I guess you were looking for 1/4 mile times from other people with 3.2s cause you already know mine has some problems.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||05-20-2000||10:01 PM||||88formula- why does the 3.4 run out at 5500? Not that I plan on turning it up that high, but could not help but wonder. It has the same crank as the 3.1, just a bigger bore- does that have something to do with it? 1fast2m6's dyno sheet showed that a 2.8 ran out of juice at somewhere around 4700, would the 3.4 also do this? I want to know MORE MORE MORE!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000008||WisconsinGT||05-20-2000||10:32 PM||||Hey 88 Formula...Can you send me that e-mail on how to check for a clogged exhaust? My e-mail is imaj@mhtc.net Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000009||88formula||05-20-2000||11:33 PM||||I got the information about that 5500 RPM redline for a stock 3.4 from a person who has one on this forum. I don’t know why the 3.4 would be factory rated at a 5500 RPM redline. I think because the 3.4 has a larger reciprocating bottom end there is a lot more stress on it than the 2.8 has with a mere three inch stroke and three and a half inch bore. The 3.4 probably would run out of breath sooner than a 2.8 would also especially using an induction system designed for a 2.8 liter engine. A 2.8-liter engine with a free breathing exhaust and slightly bigger cam will pull right up to the 6000 RPM redline and maybe even a little further with the proper valve train.<P>I hate low revving engines!<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||05-21-2000||04:16 PM||||I was at the dealership parts store talking to my Fiero buddy parts guy and he brought out the "Official" GM performance parts book. The book lists a performance camshaft for 2.8/3.1 and states that with 3.4 valve springs, makes good power up to 6000 RPM. How this translates to max RPM for the 3.4, I don't know. I assume that a more free-flowing exhaust would be needed to make good power up to those RPM levels.||||reg||14
Z||000011||88formula||05-21-2000||09:17 PM||||MMMMMMMMMMMMMMMM I bet you no one knows.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Eric||05-21-2000||10:22 PM||||So I guess I'd better get on it so you guys will know what this engine can really do.  I should say that I have an Air/Fuel gauge installed to my O2 sensor.  After talking with Shaun at the drag races, I realized my mixture is way too rich at WOT.  This leads me to believe either I have the fuel pressure turned up too high, which I doubt seriously (44 psi last time I checked), or the crimped up exhaust is disrupting the airflow to the point where the mixture is made rich.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Old Lar||05-22-2000||07:27 PM||||Isn't some of the problem with high rpm rates valve float? The springs aren't strong enough to get the valve closed, prior to the next demand on opening.||||reg||5
Z||000014||87PegasusGT||05-23-2000||03:45 PM||||3.4 does actually rev about 5 rpm higher than 2.8 according to specs. With the stock 2.8 chip though, power starts to drop off considerably after 4700. When I put the strip chip in it,it was still accelerating strong at 5500 instead of dropping off. I won't know true RPMs till I get a 3.4 chip from a forum member.||||reg||1
Z||000015||superXmotorsports||05-24-2000||09:53 AM||||I ran a 14.24 in my 3.1L GT-R which wasn't as wild as the Speedster I was building which I hoped to run 13.7 or so without nitrous.  <P>SXM||||reg||1
